{"content":"Fixed the regression in sqlparse/tokens.py. The `_TokenType.__getattr__` method had a comment saying "don't mess with dunder" but didn't actually implement that behavior - it synthesized a new `_TokenType` for any attribute access, including dunder methods like `__deepcopy__`/`__copy__`. This caused `copy.deepcopy()` (used internally, e.g. in copy issue672 test) to find a truthy `_TokenType` object for `__deepcopy__` and try to call it, raising `TypeError: '_TokenType' object is not callable`.

Fix: `__getattr__` now raises `AttributeError` when the requested name is a dunder (starts and ends with `__`), matching the existing comment's intent and letting `copy`/`pickle` etc. fall back to their normal default behavior.

Verified via `check` tool: 1 passing, 0 failing (calls_left: 2, saved remaining budget). Also manually tested `copy.deepcopy`/`copy.copy` on Token instances and on a parsed statement - all work correctly and repr output is unaffected.","tool_calls":[{"id":"final_1","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed regression in sqlparse/tokens.py: _TokenType.__getattr__ now raises AttributeError for dunder attribute names instead of synthesizing a new _TokenType object for them. This resolves the 'TypeError: _TokenType object is not callable' seen when copy.deepcopy/copy.copy (or other dunder-attribute-probing code) queried magic methods like __deepcopy__ on Token instances. Verified fix via the check tool (1 passing, 0 failing) and manual testing of copy.deepcopy/copy.copy on Token objects and parsed SQL statements."}}]}
